---
title: On the Jail Lineage
authors: [Matt Miller]
broadcast: 3
order: 1
---

The Tombs, New York City’s jail at White and Centre Streets, has occupied the same patch of ground since 1838, when John Haviland’s Egyptian Revival building rose on filled-in swampland that had once been Collect Pond, the city’s main source of freshwater until it was drained and filled decades earlier. The ground never settled fully, and neither did the building. It was replaced in 1902 by a new City Prison, connected to the Criminal Courts Building next door by an enclosed footbridge modeled, in name and in function, on Venice’s Bridge of Sighs. The original bridge in Venice connected a courtroom to a prison, built specifically so a person sentenced in one building could be moved into the other without being seen doing it, a corridor split down the middle so two people crossing at once could not even see or speak to each other. New York’s version does the same work: nobody here disappears into a building leaving from open public space. Their disappearance is isolated, into a hallway.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/matt-01.jpg" alt="Engraving of the Tombs, John Haviland’s Egyptian Revival jail, with its colonnaded entrance seen from the street" /%}

{% galeria %}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/matt-02.jpg" alt="Sepia postcard of the Bridge of Sighs in Venice, an enclosed stone bridge over a canal with gondolas below" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/matt-03.jpg" alt="Early photograph of New York’s Bridge of Sighs, the enclosed footbridge between the City Prison and the Criminal Courts Building, over a cobbled street" /%}
