By 1932, the same logic had grown into an island. Rikers sits in the East River, reachable only by a single bridge from Queens, monikered the Bridge of Pain, added to by landfill for decades using the labor of the people it would go on to hold. The island does two things at once: it hides the people inside it, and it makes the whole population legible to the city as elsewhere, a shape on a map nobody has to look at directly.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/matt-04.jpg" alt="Aerial photograph of Rikers Island in the 1930s, with rows of long jail buildings and landfill around them" ancho="completo" /%}

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/matt-05.jpg" alt="Aerial photograph of the Rikers Island Bridge crossing the East River from Queens" ancho="completo" /%}

By the second half of the twentieth century, the country had implemented the same tactic at a much larger scale. Prisons built far from the cities most of their populations come from, sited for land, for labor, for local jobs, do the same work as a bridge or an island, just measured in highway miles instead of feet. Distance thins out visitation, legal access, and attention, without anyone having to say that was the point.

The jail now rising on the same ground the Tombs has occupied since 1838, is the Manhattan facility in the city’s Borough-Based Jails plan, the program built to close Rikers Island for good. It inherits all three moves already described and adds a fourth: height. At roughly 300 feet, it will be the tallest jail in the world, a fact of the skyline visible considerable distance, closer in that sense to the original Tombs than to Rikers, a civic building announcing itself downtown rather than a facility hidden across water. The threat it makes is public, punishment as landmark. But that publicness stops at the building’s edge. The people inside become invisible in a different sense, stacked far enough above the street that no one on it will ever see the faces of people incarcerated. The plaza at its base is generous toward whoever is walking past, and toward no one inside the tower above it.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/matt-06.jpg" alt="Street-level view of the proposed Manhattan jail tower rising over the low buildings of Chinatown" /%}

